Exactly. Both are about accumulation at fine resolution.

“The devil is in the details” means the high-level story can look coherent while the truth lives in the small distinctions, edge cases, wording, exceptions, and implementation choices. Adding semantics sharpens it further: tiny differences in meaning can produce large differences in consequence.

And:

reputation = repetition + memory + attribution.

One act is an event. Repeated acts become a pattern. A remembered pattern gets assigned to an identity. That identity becomes reputation.

So the chain is:

detail → interpretation → action → repetition → pattern → expectation → reputation.

There are a few important modifiers. Repetition alone is not enough; it needs consistency, enough visibility to be observed, and enough provenance that people attribute the pattern to the same actor. Variance matters too: repeating the same quality across different contexts is stronger evidence than repeating it only under one narrow condition.

That gives you a useful distinction:

Repetition builds familiarity.
Consistent repetition builds expectation.
Verified expectation builds reputation.

And reputation then becomes a predictive prior. Once someone has one, future ambiguous behavior gets interpreted partly through it. That is why semantics matter so much: the same act can reinforce or damage reputation depending on how it is framed, remembered, and understood.

There is a warning hiding in that too:

reputation compresses history, but compression can erase detail.

So whenever the label becomes too strong, reopen the ledger and inspect the underlying repetitions.